  "compare": "exact",
  "referenceSolution": "def spiral_order(matrix: List[List[int]]) -> List[int]:\n    result = []\n    top, bottom = 0, len(matrix) - 1\n    left, right = 0, len(matrix[0]) - 1\n    while top <= bottom and left <= right:\n        for j in range(left, right + 1):\n            result.append(matrix[top][j])\n        for i in range(top + 1, bottom + 1):\n            result.append(matrix[i][right])\n        if top < bottom:\n            for j in range(right - 1, left - 1, -1):\n                result.append(matrix[bottom][j])\n        if left < right:\n            for i in range(bottom - 1, top, -1):\n                result.append(matrix[i][left])\n        top, bottom, left, right = top + 1, bottom - 1, left + 1, right - 1\n    return result\n",
  "targetComplexity": {"time": "O(m * n)", "space": "O(1) extra, not counting the output"},
  "hints": [
    "Trace the spiral by hand on a 3 x 4 grid. Which four moves repeat, and what changes after each lap?",
    "Think of the outer ring as one lap. Once it's done, what remains is a smaller grid with the same problem.",
    "Track the four edges that are still unvisited: top row, bottom row, left column, right column. How does each one shrink after it is walked?",
    "The tricky case is a single remaining row or column. After walking the top row, walking back along the bottom row would repeat cells. What check prevents that?",
    "Keep top, bottom, left and right bounds. Walk right along top then move top down, walk down along right then move right in, and only walk back along the bottom or up the left side if a row or column is still left."
  ],
